<handout component="day26" xml:id="day-26-lecture">
    <title>Day 26</title>

    <introduction>
        <p>
            9.3: Support Vector Machines (SVMs)
        </p>
    </introduction>

    <objectives>
    
  <ul>
    <li>
      <p>How is nonlinearity introduced in Support Vector Machines?
      <ul>
        <li><p>You should be able to describe how kernels work conceptually and mathematically.</p></li>
      </ul>
      </p>
    </li>
    <li><p>What type of kernel makes a Support Vector Machine equivalent to a Support Vector Classifier?</p></li>
    <li><p>What other kernels are there?</p></li>
    <li><p>What type of kernel leads to more local behavior?</p></li>
    <li>
      <p>How do you use Support Vector Machines in Python?
      <ul>
        <li><p>You should be able to fit a Support Vector Machine using different kernels covered in class.</p></li>
        <li><p>You should be able to select the appropriate hyperparameter that optimizes the bias-variance trade-off.</p></li>
      </ul>
      </p>
    </li>
  </ul>
        
    </objectives>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>Enlarging the feature space</title>

        <image source="figures/enlarging_feature_space" width="80%">
        <shortdescription>Non-linearly separable data drawn on the plane of predictors X1 and X2 on the left panel, and the same data linearly separable by a hyperplane when enlarging the feature space to the sum of the squared predictors.</shortdescription>
        </image>

        <p>
            Problem: Data not linearly separable. What if we enlarge the feature space?
        </p>

        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Enlarge using <m>Z=X^2 + Y^2</m>?
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        The data becomes linearly separable in the enlarged space!
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
        

    <p>
  Say we want to enlarge the feature space using <m>2p</m> features:

<md>X_1, X_1^2, X_2, X_2^2, \dots, X_p, X_p^2</md>
</p>

<p>
  Optimization becomes:
</p>

<p>
<md>
  <mrow>\amp \underset{\beta_0, \beta_{11}, \beta_{12}, \dots, \beta_{p1}, \beta_{p2}, \epsilon_1, \dots, \epsilon_n, M}{\text{maximize}} \quad M</mrow>
  <mrow>\amp \text{subject to } y_i \left( \beta_0 + \sum_{j=1}^p \beta_{j1}x_{ij} + \sum_{j=1}^p \beta_{j2}x_{ij}^2 \right) \geq M(1 - \epsilon_i),</mrow>
  <mrow>\amp \sum_{i=1}^n \epsilon_i \leq C, \quad \epsilon_i \geq 0, \quad \sum_{j=1}^p \sum_{k=1}^2 \beta_{jk}^2 = 1.</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <p>
    This becomes unwieldly if we have to check, say every degree 2 monomial <m>X_iX_j</m>, so need something more efficient.         
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        What if we want to enlarge the feature space using non-polynomial functions. How do we do that?
      </p>
    </li>
<li>
  <p>
    The support vector machine allows enlarging the feature space in a computationally efficient way.
  </p>
</li>
  </ul>
</p>
    </paragraphs>

  <paragraphs>
    <title></title>
    <p>
      Let <m>\mathbf{x}=(x_1,\ldots, x_p)</m> be a new test observation. The SVC can be written as
      <md>
      <mrow>f(x) \amp= \beta_0 + \beta_1 x_1 + \ldots + \beta_p x_p</mrow>
      <mrow>     \amp= \beta_0 + \sum\limits_{j=1}^p{\beta_j x_j}</mrow>
      <mrow>     \amp= \beta_0 +  [x_1,\ldots,x_p]\cdot [beta_1,\ldots,\beta_p]</mrow>
      <mrow>     \amp= \beta_0 + \langle\mathbf{x},\boldsymbol{\beta} \rangle </mrow>
      </md>      
    </p>

    <p>
      The same SVC can be equivalently written observation weights <m>\alpha_i</m> as
      <md>
      <mrow>f(x) \amp= \beta_0 + (\sum\limits_{i=1}^n{\alpha_i x_{i1}}) x_1 + (\sum\limits_{i=1}^n{\alpha_i x_{i2}}) x_2 + \ldots + (\sum\limits_{i=1}^n{\alpha_i x_{ip}}) x_p</mrow>
        <mrow>   \amp= \beta_0 + \sum\limits_{j=1}^p{(\sum\limits_{i=1}^n{\alpha_i x_{ij}}) x_j} </mrow>
        <mrow> \amp= \beta_0 + \sum\limits_{i=1}^n{\alpha_i \langle x, x_i\rangle}</mrow>
      </md>
      
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <paragraphs>
    <title>Enlarging the feature space via kernels</title>
    <p>
      The kernel trick: Assume we have features <m>(x_1,x_2,x_3)</m> and observations <m>y_1, y_2, y_3</m>. We want to enlarge the feature space to 
      <md>
        \varphi(\mathbf{x})=(x_1 x_1 ,x_1 x_2,x_1 x_3,x_2 x_1, x_2 x_2, x_2 x_3, x_3 x_1,x_3 x_2, x_3 x_3).
      </md>      
    </p>
    <p>
      Approach 1: Compute the enlarged feature space for each observation, then perform the inner product.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>


</handout>